            "To find Planck's constant, we can use the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\lambda = \\frac{hc}{eV}\n\\]\n\nwhere $h$ is Planck's constant, $c$ is the speed of light, and $eV$ is the energy of the emitted photoelectron.\n\nGiven:\n- $h = 6.4 \\times 10^{-34} \\mathrm{J} \\mathrm{~s}$\n- $c = 3 \\times 10^{8} \\mathrm{~m} \\mathrm{~s}^{-1}$\n- $eV = 1.6 \\times 10^{-19} \\mathrm{C} \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19} \\mathrm{C} = 2.56 \\times 10^{-38} \\mathrm{J}$\n\nWe can rearrange the formula to solve for $h$:\n\n\\[\nh = \\frac{\\lambda \\cdot c \\cdot eV}{1}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the given values:\n\n\\[\nh = \\frac{0.3 \\times 3 \\times 10^{8} \\mathrm{~m} \\mathrm{~s}^{-1} \\times 2.56 \\times 10^{-38} \\mathrm{J}}{1}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nh = \\frac{0.3 \\times 3 \\times 2.56 \\times 10^{-38}}{1}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nh = 0.3 \\times 3 \\times 2.56 \\times 10^{-38}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nh = 0.3 \\times 7.68 \\times 10^{-38}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nh = 2.304 \\times 10^{-38} \\mathrm{J}\n\\]\n\nConverting to the correct unit of $J \\mathrm{~s}$:\n\n\\[\nh = 2.304 \\times 10^{-38} \\times 3 \\times 10^{8} \\mathrm{~s}^{-1}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nh = 6.912 \\times 10^{-30} \\mathrm{J} \\mathrm{~s}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{B}\n\\]",
