          "question": "One mole of an ideal gas at $300 \\mathrm{~K}$ in thermal contact with surroundings expands isothermally from $1.0 \\mathrm{~L}$ to $2.0 \\mathrm{~L}$ against a constant pressure of $3.0 \\mathrm{~atm}$. In this process, the change in entropy of surroundings $\\left(\\Delta S_{\\text {surr }}\\right)$ in $\\mathrm{J} \\mathrm{K}^{-1}$ is\n\n(1 $\\mathrm{L} \\operatorname{atm}=101.3 \\mathrm{~J})$\n\n(A) 5.763\n\n(B) 1.013\n\n(C) -1.013\n\n(D) -5.763",
          "model_outputs": [
            "To solve this problem, we will use the formula for entropy change of an ideal gas:\n\n$\\Delta S = nR\\ln\\left(\\frac{V_2}{V_1}\\right)$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of moles of the gas, $R$ is the ideal gas constant, and $V_1$ and $V_2$ are the initial and final volumes of the gas, respectively.\n\nGiven:\n- $n = 1$ (one mole of gas)\n- $R = 8.314 \\mathrm{~J} \\mathrm{K}^{-1} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\n- $V_1 = 1.0 \\mathrm{~L}$\n- $V_2 = 2.0 \\mathrm{~L}$\n- $P = 3.0 \\mathrm{~atm} = 101.3 \\mathrm{~J}$\n\nFirst, we need to convert the volume from liters to cubic meters:\n\n$V_1 = 1.0 \\mathrm{~L} = 0.001 \\mathrm{~m}^3$\n$V_2 = 2.0 \\mathrm{~L} = 0.002 \\mathrm{~m}^3$\n\nNow, we can plug these values into the entropy change formula:\n\n$\\Delta S = 1 \\times 8.314 \\mathrm{~J} \\mathrm{K}^{-1} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1} \\times \\ln\\left(\\frac{0.002}{0.001}\\right)$\n\n$\\Delta S = 8.314 \\mathrm{~J} \\mathrm{K}^{-1} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1} \\times \\ln(2)$\n\n$\\Delta S = 8.314 \\times 0.6931 \\approx 5.763 \\mathrm{~J} \\mathrm{K}^{-1}$\n\nTherefore, the change in entropy of the surroundings is approximately $5.763 \\mathrm{~J} \\mathrm{K}^{-1}$.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{A}$.",
