          "index": 32,
          "type": "Integer",
          "question": "The mole fraction of a solute in a solution is 0.1 . At $298 \\mathrm{~K}$, molarity of this solution is the same as its molality. Density of this solution at $298 \\mathrm{~K}$ is $2.0 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~cm}^{-3}$. What is the ratio of the molecular weights of the solute and solvent, $\\left(\\frac{M W_{\\text {solute }}}{M W_{\\text {solvent }}}\\right)$?",
          "model_outputs": [
            "\n1. **Understanding the Problem:**\n   - We are given the mole fraction of a solute, $x = 0.1$.\n   - The molarity of the solution at $298 \\mathrm{~K}$ is the same as its molality.\n   - The density of the solution at $298 \\mathrm{~K}$ is $2.0 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~cm}^{-3}$.\n   - We need to find the ratio of the molecular weights of the solute and solvent, $\\left(\\frac{M W_{\\text{solute}}}{M W_{\\text{solvent}}}\\right)$.\n\n2. **Molarity and Molality:**\n   - Molarity (M) is defined as the number of moles of solute per liter of solution.\n   - Molality (m) is defined as the number of moles of solute per kilogram of solvent.\n\n3. **Given Information:**\n   - Mole fraction of solute, $x = 0.1$.\n   - Molarity at $298 \\mathrm{~K}$ is the same as molality.\n   - Density of the solution at $298 \\mathrm{~K}$ is $2.0 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~cm}^{-3}$.\n\n4. **Setting Up the Equation:**\n   - Since the molarity is the same as molality, we have:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{n_{\\text{solute}}}{n_{\\text{solvent}}} = \\frac{m_{\\text{solute}}}{m_{\\text{solvent}}}\n     \\]\n   - Here, $n_{\\text{solute}}$ is the number of moles of solute, $n_{\\text{solvent}}$ is the number of moles of solvent, $m_{\\text{solute}}$ is the molality of the solute, and $m_{\\text{solvent}}$ is the molality of the solvent.\n\n5. **Using the Density:**\n   - The density of the solution is given by:\n     \\[\n     \\rho = \\frac{n_{\\text{solute}} \\cdot M_{\\text{solute}}}{n_{\\text{solvent}} \\cdot M_{\\text{solvent}}}\n     \\]\n   - Rearranging this equation to solve for the ratio of the molecular weights:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{M_{\\text{solute}}}{M_{\\text{solvent}}} = \\frac{n_{\\text{solvent}} \\cdot \\rho}{n_{\\text{solute}} \\cdot \\rho}\n     \\]\n\n6. **Substituting the Given Values:**\n   - Given density, $\\rho = 2.0 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~cm}^{-3}$.\n   - Molarity, $n_{\\text{solute}} = 0.1 \\times \\frac{M_{\\text{solute}}}{M_{\\text{solvent}}}$.\n   - Molarity, $n_{\\text{solvent}} = \\frac{m_{\\text{solvent}}}{M_{\\text{solvent}}}$.\n\n7. **Simplifying the Equation:**\n   - Substitute $n_{\\text{solute}}$ and $n_{\\text{solvent}}$ in the equation:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{M_{\\text{solute}}}{M_{\\text{solvent}}} = \\frac{\\frac{m_{\\text{solvent}}}{M_{\\text{solvent}}} \\cdot 2.0 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~cm}^{-3}}{\\frac{0.1 \\times \\frac{M_{\\text{solute}}}{M_{\\text{solvent}}}} \\cdot 2.0 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~cm}^{-3}}\n     \\]\n   - Simplifying further:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{M_{\\text{solute}}}{M_{\\text{solvent}}} = \\frac{m_{\\text{solvent}}}{0.1 \\times \\frac{M_{\\text{solute}}}{M_{\\text{solvent}}}}\n     \\]\n   - Canceling out the common terms:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{M_{\\text{solute}}}{M_{\\text{solvent}}} = \\frac{m_{\\text{solvent}}}{0.1 \\times \\frac{M_{\\text{solute}}}{M_{\\text{solvent}}}}\n     \\]\n   - Simplifying the fraction:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{M_{\\text{solute}}}{M_{\\text{solvent}}} = \\frac{m_{\\text{solvent}}}{0.1 \\times \\frac{M_{\\text{solute}}}{M_{\\text{solvent}}}} = \\frac{m_{\\text{solvent}}}{0.1}\n     \\]\n\n8. **Final Ratio:**\n   - The ratio of the molecular weights of the solute and solvent is:\n     \\[\n     \\left(\\frac{M_{\\text{solute}}}{M_{\\text{solvent}}}\\right) = \\frac{m_{\\text{solvent}}}{0.1}\n     \\]\n\n9. **Conclusion:**\n   - The ratio of the molecular weights of the solute and solvent is $\\frac{m_{\\text{solvent}}}{0.1}$.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{m_{\\text{solvent}}}{0.1}}\n\\]",
