            "\n1. **Setting up the system of equations**:\n   We are given a system of linear equations:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   x = 2x + 3x \\\\\n   x^{2} = 4x^{2} + 9x^{2} \\\\\n   1 + x^{3} = 1 + 8x^{3} + 27x^{3}\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying each equation, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   0 = 4x \\\\\n   0 = 10x^{2} \\\\\n   0 = 28x^{3}\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Solving for $x$**:\n   From the first equation, we have:\n   \\[\n   0 = 4x \\implies x = 0\n   \\]\n   From the second equation, we have:\n   \\[\n   0 = 10x^{2} \\implies x^{2} = 0 \\implies x = 0\n   \\]\n   From the third equation, we have:\n   \\[\n   0 = 28x^{3} \\implies x^{3} = 0 \\implies x = 0\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the only solution is $x = 0$.\n\n3. **Conclusion**:\n   The only value of $x$ that satisfies the given system of equations is $x = 0$.\n\nThus, the total number of distinct $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ for which $\\left|\\begin{array}{ccc}x & x^{2} & 1+x^{3} \\\\ 2 x & 4 x^{2} & 1+8 x^{3} \\\\ 3 x & 9 x^{2} & 1+27 x^{3}\\end{array}\\right|=10$ is $\\boxed{1}$.",
