oefficient of linear thermal expansion of wire $R S$ is twice that of wire $P Q$, the change in length of wire $R S$ is twice that of wire $P Q$. Therefore, the change in length of wire $P Q$ is:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta L = 2 \\times 39 \\mathrm{~mm} = 78 \\mathrm{~mm}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the final length of wire $P Q$:**\n   The final length of wire $P Q$ is:\n   \\[\n   L_f = L_0 + \\Delta L = 1 \\mathrm{~m} + 78 \\mathrm{~mm} = 1.78 \\mathrm{~m}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Calculate the change in length of wire $P Q$:**\n   The change in length of wire $P Q$ is:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta L = L_f - L_0 = 1.78 \\mathrm{~m} - 1 \\mathrm{~m} = 0.78 \\mathrm{~m}\n   \\]\n   Converting this to millimeters:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta L = 0.78 \\mathrm{~m} \\times 1000 \\mathrm{~mm/m} = 780 \\mathrm{~mm}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the change in length of wire $P Q$ is $780 \\mathrm{~mm}$.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{780 \\mathrm{~mm}}$.",
