            "\n1. **Understanding the Problem:**\n   - We are given a face-centered cubic (FCC) crystal structure with a cell edge of $400 \\mathrm{pm}$.\n   - The density of the substance in the crystal is $8 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~cm}^{-3}$.\n   - We need to find the number of atoms present in $256 \\mathrm{~g}$ of the crystal.\n\n2. **Conversion of Units:**\n   - The density is given in grams per cubic centimeter (g/cm³).\n   - The volume of the crystal is given in cubic meters (cm³).\n   - We need to convert the volume from cubic centimeters to cubic meters.\n\n3. **Volume of the Cube:**\n   - The volume of a cube is given by the formula \\( V = s^3 \\), where \\( s \\) is the length of one side of the cube.\n   - Given the edge length \\( s = 400 \\mathrm{pm} = 0.4 \\mathrm{~cm} \\).\n   - Therefore, the volume \\( V = (0.4 \\mathrm{~cm})^3 = 0.064 \\mathrm{~cm}^3 \\).\n\n4. **Conversion of Volume to Cubic Meters:**\n   - Since there are $10^3$ cubic meters in a cubic centimeter, we convert the volume from cubic centimeters to cubic meters:\n   \\[\n   V_{\\text{m}} = \\frac{V}{10^3} = \\frac{0.064 \\mathrm{~cm}^3}{10^3} = 0.000064 \\mathrm{~cm}^3\n   \\]\n   - Converting back to cubic meters:\n   \\[\n   V_{\\text{m}} = 0.000064 \\mathrm{~cm}^3 \\times \\frac{1 \\mathrm{~cm}^3}{10^3} = 6.4 \\times 10^{-7} \\mathrm{~m}^3\n   \\]\n\n5. **Number of Atoms in the Cube:**\n   - The density of the substance is given as $8 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~cm}^{-3}$.\n   - The number of atoms \\( N \\) in the cube can be calculated using the formula:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Density} = \\frac{\\text{Number of Atoms}}{\\text{Volume of the Cube}}\n   \\]\n   - Rearranging to solve for the number of atoms:\n   \\[\n   N = \\text{Density} \\times \\text{Volume of the Cube}\n   \\]\n   - Substituting the values:\n   \\[\n   N = 8 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~cm}^{-3} \\times 6.4 \\times 10^{-7} \\mathrm{~m}^3\n   \\]\n   - Converting the density to grams per cubic meter:\n   \\[\n   8 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~cm}^{-3} = 8 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~cm}^{-3}\n   \\]\n   - Therefore:\n   \\[\n   N = 8 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~cm}^{-3} \\times 6.4 \\times 10^{-7} \\mathrm{~m}^3 = 5.12 \\times 10^{-10} \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~m}^{-3}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Conversion to Atoms:**\n   - To find the number of atoms, we need to convert the density to grams per atom:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Density in g/atom} = \\frac{5.12 \\times 10^{-10} \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~m}^{-3}}{6.4 \\times 10^{-7} \\mathrm{~m}^3} = 8 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~m}^{-3}\n   \\]\n   - Converting back to atoms:\n   \\[\n   N = 8 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~m}^{-3} \\times 6.4 \\times 10^{-7} \\mathrm{~m}^3 = 5.12 \\times 10^{-10} \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~m}^{-3}\n   \\]\n   - Converting to atoms:\n   \\[\n   N = 5.12 \\times 10^{-10} \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~m}^{-3} \\times 6.4 \\times 10^{-7} \\mathrm{~m}^3 = 3.2 \\times 10^{-17} \\mathrm{~atoms}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Conclusion:**\n   - The number of atoms present in $256 \\mathrm{~g}$ of the crystal is $3.2 \\times 10^{-17} \\mathrm{~atoms}$.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{3.2 \\times 10^{-17} \\mathrm{~atoms}}\n\\]",
