            "To solve this problem, we start by considering the relationship between the density $\\rho$ and the velocity $v$ of a point on the surface of the expanding sphere. Since the density remains uniform throughout the volume, we can use the fact that the velocity of a point on the surface of a sphere is proportional to the radius of the sphere.\n\nGiven that the velocity $v$ is proportional to the radius $R$ of the sphere, we can write:\n\n\\[ v = kR \\]\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nNow, we need to find the rate of change of the density $\\left(\\frac{1}{\\rho} \\frac{d \\rho}{d t}\\right)$. Since the density remains uniform throughout the volume, we can use the fact that the rate of change of density is proportional to the rate of change of the volume.\n\nThe rate of change of the volume of a sphere is given by the formula:\n\n\\[ \\frac{dV}{dt} = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi R^3 \\frac{dR}{dt} \\]\n\nSince the density remains uniform, the rate of change of the volume is also proportional to the rate of change of the radius $R$. Therefore, we can write:\n\n\\[ \\frac{dV}{dt} = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi R^3 \\frac{dR}{dt} = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi \\left(\\frac{1}{\\rho} \\frac{d \\rho}{d t}\\right) R^3 \\]\n\nNow, we can equate the two expressions for the rate of change of the volume:\n\n\\[ \\frac{4}{3} \\pi \\left(\\frac{1}{\\rho} \\frac{d \\rho}{d t}\\right) R^3 = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi \\left(\\frac{1}{\\rho} \\frac{d \\rho}{d t}\\right) R^3 \\]\n\nThis equation is true for any value of $R$, so we can conclude that the rate of change of the density is indeed constant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{C} \\]",
