          "question": "A person measures the depth of a well by measuring the time interval between dropping a stone and receiving the sound of impact with the bottom of the well. The error in his measurement of time is $\\delta T=0.01$ seconds and he measures the depth of the well to be $L=20$ meters. Take the acceleration due to gravity $g=10 \\mathrm{~ms}^{-2}$ and the velocity of sound is $300 \\mathrm{~ms}^{-1}$. Then the fractional error in the measurement, $\\delta L / L$, is closest to\n\n[A] $0.2 \\%$\n\n[B] $1 \\%$\n\n[C] $3 \\%$\n\n[D] $5 \\%$",
          "model_outputs": [
            "To solve this problem, we need to calculate the fractional error in the measurement of the depth of the well, $\\delta L / L$. We are given the following values:\n\n- $\\delta T = 0.01$ seconds (error in time measurement)\n- $L = 20$ meters (depth of the well)\n- $g = 10 \\mathrm{~ms}^{-2}$ (acceleration due to gravity)\n- $v_s = 300 \\mathrm{~ms}^{-1}$ (velocity of sound)\n\nFirst, we need to calculate the time interval $T$ between the dropping of the stone and the reception of the sound of impact. We can use the formula for the time interval between two events:\n\n\\[ T = \\frac{L}{v_s} \\]\n\nSubstituting the given values:\n\n\\[ T = \\frac{20 \\text{ meters}}{300 \\text{ ms}^{-1}} = \\frac{20}{0.03} \\text{ seconds} \\]\n\n\\[ T = 666.67 \\text{ seconds} \\]\n\nNow, we can calculate the fractional error in the measurement of the depth of the well, $\\delta L / L$:\n\n\\[ \\delta L / L = \\frac{\\delta T}{L} \\]\n\nSubstituting the given values:\n\n\\[ \\delta L / L = \\frac{0.01}{20} \\]\n\n\\[ \\delta L / L = 0.0005 \\]\n\nTo express this as a percentage, we multiply by 100:\n\n\\[ \\delta L / L = 0.0005 \\times 100 = 0.05 \\]\n\nTherefore, the fractional error in the measurement, $\\delta L / L$, is closest to $0.05$.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{0.05}$.",
