          "question": "The instantaneous voltages at three terminals marked $X, Y$ and $Z$ are given by\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n& V_{X}=V_{0} \\sin \\omega t, \\\\\n\n& V_{Y}=V_{0} \\sin \\left(\\omega t+\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right) \\text { and } \\\\\n\n& V_{Z}=V_{0} \\sin \\left(\\omega t+\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\right) .\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAn ideal voltmeter is configured to read $\\mathrm{rms}$ value of the potential difference between its terminals. It is connected between points $X$ and $Y$ and then between $Y$ and $Z$. The reading(s) of the voltmeter will be\n\n[A] $\\quad V_{X Y}^{r m s}=V_{0} \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}$\n\n[B] $\\quad V_{Y Z}^{r m s}=V_{0} \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}$\n\n[C] $\\quad V_{X Y}^{r m s}=V_{0}$\n\n[D] independent of the choice of the two terminals",
          "model_outputs": [
            "To solve this problem, we need to calculate the instantaneous voltage at each terminal and then find the rms value of the difference between the two terminals.\n\n1. **Calculate the instantaneous voltage at $X$:**\n   \\[\n   V_{X} = V_{0} \\sin \\omega t\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the instantaneous voltage at $Y$:**\n   \\[\n   V_{Y} = V_{0} \\sin \\left(\\omega t + \\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the instantaneous voltage at $Z$:**\n   \\[\n   V_{Z} = V_{0} \\sin \\left(\\omega t + \\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the difference in instantaneous voltage between $X$ and $Y$:**\n   \\[\n   V_{X Y} = V_{0} \\sin \\omega t - V_{0} \\sin \\left(\\omega t + \\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right)\n   \\]\n   Using the sine difference identity:\n   \\[\n   V_{X Y} = V_{0} \\left[ \\sin \\omega t - \\sin \\left(\\omega t + \\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right) \\right]\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = V_{0} \\left[ \\sin \\omega t - \\left( \\sin \\omega t \\cos \\frac{2 \\pi}{3} + \\cos \\omega t \\sin \\frac{2 \\pi}{3} \\right) \\right]\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = V_{0} \\left[ \\sin \\omega t - \\left( \\sin \\omega t \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) + \\cos \\omega t \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right) \\right]\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = V_{0} \\left[ \\sin \\omega t + \\frac{1}{2} \\sin \\omega t - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cos \\omega t \\right]\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = V_{0} \\left[ \\frac{3}{2} \\sin \\omega t - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cos \\omega t \\right]\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = V_{0} \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^2 + \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^2} \\sin \\left(\\omega t - \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = V_{0} \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}} \\sin \\left(\\omega t - \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the difference in instantaneous voltage between $Y$ and $Z$:**\n   \\[\n   V_{Y Z} = V_{0} \\sin \\left(\\omega t + \\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\right) - V_{0} \\sin \\omega t\n   \\]\n   Using the sine difference identity:\n   \\[\n   V_{Y Z} = V_{0} \\left[