htarrow{O Q} - \\overrightarrow{O R} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{O P} = \\overrightarrow{O Q} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{O S} - \\overrightarrow{O R} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{O S}\n     \\]\n   - This can be rewritten as:\n     \\[\n     \\overrightarrow{O P} \\cdot (\\overrightarrow{O Q} - \\overrightarrow{O R}) = \\overrightarrow{O Q} \\cdot (\\overrightarrow{O S} - \\overrightarrow{O R})\n     \\]\n\n5. **Analyze the implications:**\n   - Since $\\overrightarrow{O P} \\cdot (\\overrightarrow{O Q} - \\overrightarrow{O R}) = \\overrightarrow{O Q} \\cdot (\\overrightarrow{O S} - \\overrightarrow{O R})$, we can conclude that:\n     \\[\n     \\overrightarrow{O Q} - \\overrightarrow{O R} = \\overrightarrow{O S} - \\overrightarrow{O R}\n     \\]\n   - Simplifying this, we get:\n     \\[\n     \\overrightarrow{O Q} = \\overrightarrow{O S}\n     \\]\n\n6. **Conclusion:**\n   - Since $\\overrightarrow{O Q} = \\overrightarrow{O S}$, the point $S$ is the midpoint of the segment $\\overrightarrow{O Q}$.\n   - Therefore, the triangle $P Q R$ has $S$ as its centroid.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{A}}\n\\]",
