          "question": "Liquids $\\mathbf{A}$ and $\\mathbf{B}$ form ideal solution over the entire range of composition. At temperature $\\mathrm{T}$, equimolar binary solution of liquids $\\mathbf{A}$ and $\\mathbf{B}$ has vapour pressure 45 Torr. At the same temperature, a new solution of $\\mathbf{A}$ and $\\mathbf{B}$ having mole fractions $x_{A}$ and $x_{B}$, respectively, has vapour pressure of 22.5 Torr. What is the value of $x_{A} / x_{B}$ in the new solution? (given that the vapour pressure of pure liquid $\\mathbf{A}$ is 20 Torr at temperature $\\mathrm{T}$ )",
          "model_outputs": [
            "To solve this problem, we need to use the relationship between the vapor pressure of a solution and the mole fractions of the components. The vapor pressure of a solution is given by the following equation:\n\n\\[ P = X_A \\cdot P_A + X_B \\cdot P_B \\]\n\nwhere $P$ is the vapor pressure, $X_A$ and $X_B$ are the mole fractions of components $\\mathbf{A}$ and $\\mathbf{B}$, respectively, and $P_A$ and $P_B$ are the vapor pressures of pure liquids $\\mathbf{A}$ and $\\mathbf{B}$, respectively.\n\nGiven:\n- $P = 45$ Torr\n- $P_A = 20$ Torr\n- $P_B = 22.5$ Torr\n- $X_A = x_A$\n- $X_B = x_B$\n\nWe can plug these values into the equation:\n\n\\[ 45 = x_A \\cdot 20 + x_B \\cdot 22.5 \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the value of $x_A / x_B$. To do this, we can rearrange the equation to isolate $x_A$ in terms of $x_B$:\n\n\\[ x_A \\cdot 20 = 45 - x_B \\cdot 22.5 \\]\n\n\\[ x_A = \\frac{45 - x_B \\cdot 22.5}{20} \\]\n\nNow, we can divide both sides by $x_B$ to get:\n\n\\[ \\frac{x_A}{x_B} = \\frac{45 - x_B \\cdot 22.5}{20x_B} \\]\n\nHowever, we don't have the value of $x_B$ directly. But we can use the fact that the mole fractions of the components in a solution must sum to 1:\n\n\\[ x_A + x_B = 1 \\]\n\nWe can substitute $x_A$ from the rearranged equation:\n\n\\[ x_B = 1 - x_A \\]\n\nNow, we can substitute $x_A$ from the rearranged equation:\n\n\\[ x_B = 1 - \\frac{45 - x_B \\cdot 22.5}{20x_B} \\]\n\nThis equation is still complex, but we can simplify it by multiplying both sides by $20x_B$:\n\n\\[ 20x_B^2 = 20x_B - 45 + x_B \\cdot 22.5 \\]\n\n\\[ 20x_B^2 - x_B \\cdot 22.5 - 20x_B + 45 = 0 \\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in terms of $x_B$. We can solve it using the quadratic formula:\n\n\\[ x_B = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\]\n\nwhere $a = 20$, $b = -22.5$, and $c = 45$.\n\n\\[ x_B = \\frac{22.5 \\pm \\sqrt{(-22.5)^2 - 4 \\cdot 20 \\cdot 45}}{2 \\cdot 20} \\]\n\n\\[ x_B = \\frac{22.5 \\pm \\sqrt{506.25 - 3600}}{40} \\]\n\n\\[ x_B = \\frac{22.5 \\pm \\