          "type": "Numeric",
          "question": "A particle, of mass $10^{-3} \\mathrm{~kg}$ and charge $1.0 \\mathrm{C}$, is initially at rest. At time $t=0$, the particle comes under the influence of an electric field $\\vec{E}(t)=E_{0} \\sin \\omega t \\hat{i}$, where $E_{0}=1.0 \\mathrm{~N}^{-1}$ and $\\omega=10^{3} \\mathrm{rad} \\mathrm{s}^{-1}$. Consider the effect of only the electrical force on the particle. Then what is the maximum speed, in $m s^{-1}$, attained by the particle at subsequent times?",
          "model_outputs": [
            "\n1. **Initial Conditions and Setup**:\n   - The particle has a mass of $10^{-3} \\mathrm{~kg}$ and a charge of $1.0 \\mathrm{C}$.\n   - The electric field is given by $\\vec{E}(t) = E_{0} \\sin \\omega t \\hat{i}$, where $E_{0} = 1.0 \\mathrm{~N}^{-1}$ and $\\omega = 10^{3} \\mathrm{rad} \\mathrm{s}^{-1}$.\n\n2. **Electric Force Calculation**:\n   - The electric force acting on the particle is given by the product of the charge and the electric field:\n     \\[\n     F(t) = q \\vec{E}(t) = 1.0 \\mathrm{C} \\cdot E_{0} \\sin \\omega t \\hat{i}\n     \\]\n   - Substituting the given values, we get:\n     \\[\n     F(t) = 1.0 \\cdot 1.0 \\cdot 10^{-3} \\sin(10^{3} t) \\hat{i}\n     \\]\n\n3. **Velocity Calculation**:\n   - The velocity of the particle is the time derivative of the electric force:\n     \\[\n     \\vec{v}(t) = \\frac{dF(t)}{dt} = \\frac{d}{dt} \\left(1.0 \\cdot 10^{-3} \\sin(10^{3} t) \\hat{i}\\right)\n     \\]\n   - Using the chain rule, we find:\n     \\[\n     \\vec{v}(t) = -10^{3} \\cdot 10^{-3} \\cdot 10^{3} \\sin(10^{3} t) \\hat{i} = -10^{3} \\sin(10^{3} t) \\hat{i}\n     \\]\n\n4. **Maximum Speed Calculation**:\n   - The maximum speed is the amplitude of the velocity function, which is the maximum value of $\\sin(10^{3} t)$:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Maximum speed} = 10^{3}\n     \\]\n\n5. **Conclusion**:\n   - The maximum speed attained by the particle at subsequent times is $10^{3} \\mathrm{~m s}^{-1}$.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{10^{3} \\mathrm{~m s}^{-1}}\n\\]",
