\nNow, we need to find the shunt resistance required to convert the galvanometer into an ammeter capable of measuring current in the range $0-1.0 \\mathrm{~A}$. The shunt resistance \\( R_s \\) is given by the formula:\n\n\\[ R_s = \\frac{I_{max} - I_{min}}{I_{max} - I_{min}} \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( I_{max} \\) is the maximum current,\n- \\( I_{min} \\) is the minimum current.\n\nGiven values:\n- \\( I_{max} = 1.0 \\) A,\n- \\( I_{min} = 0 \\) A.\n\nPlugging these values into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[ R_s = \\frac{1.0 - 0}{1.0 - 0} \\]\n\n\\[ R_s = \\frac{1.0}{1.0} \\]\n\n\\[ R_s = 1.0 \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of the shunt resistance required to convert the galvanometer into an ammeter capable of measuring current in the range $0-1.0 \\mathrm{~A}$ is $\\boxed{1.0 \\mathrm{~ohm}}$.",
            "To solve this problem, we need to determine the value of the shunt resistance required to convert the galvanometer into an ammeter capable of measuring current in the range $0-1.0 \\mathrm{~A}$.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the torque produced by the galvanometer. The torque is given by the formula:\n\n\\[ \\tau = N \\cdot B \\cdot I \\cdot A \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( N \\) is the number of turns,\n- \\( B \\) is the magnetic field,\n- \\( I \\) is the current,\n- \\( A \\) is the area of the coil.\n\nGiven values:\n- \\( N = 50 \\) turns,\n- \\( B = 0.02 \\) T,\n- \\( I = 1.0 \\) A,\n- \\( A = 2 \\times 10^{-4} \\) m\\(^2\\).\n\nPlugging these values into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[ \\tau = 50 \\cdot 0.02 \\cdot 1.0 \\cdot 2 \\times 10^{-4} \\]\n\n\\[ \\tau = 50 \\cdot 0.02 \\cdot 1.0 \\cdot 2 \\times 10^{-4} \\]\n\n\\[ \\tau = 50 \\cdot 0.02 \\cdot 2 \\times 10^{-4} \\]\n\n\\[ \\tau = 50 \\cdot 0.04 \\cdot 10^{-4} \\]\n\n\\[ \\tau = 2 \\cdot 10^{-4} \\]\n\nThis torque is the amount of torque that must be applied to the galvanometer to cause a full scale deflection.\n\nNext, we need to determine the current that will cause a full scale deflection. The full scale deflection occurs when the coil rotates by $0.2 \\mathrm{rad}$. The relationship between the current and the deflection angle is given by the formula:\n\n\\[ \\theta = \\frac{2\\pi}{T} \\cdot I \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( \\theta \\) is the deflection angle,\n- \\( T \\) is the torsion constant of the suspension wire.\n\nGiven values:\n- \\( \\theta = 0.2 \\) rad,\n- \\( T = 10^{-4} \\) Nm\\(^{-1}\\).\n\nPlugging these values into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[ 0.2 = \\frac{2\\pi}{10^{-4}} \\cdot I \\]\n\n\\[ 0.2 = \\frac{2\\pi \\cdot 10^{-4}}{10^{-4}} \\cdot I \\]\n\n\\[ 0.2 = 2\\pi \\cdot I \\]\n\n\\[ I = \\frac{0.2}{2\\pi} \\]\n\n\\[ I = \\frac{0.2}{2 \\cdot 3.14159} \\]\n\n\\[ I = \\frac{0.2}{6.28318} \\]\n\n\\[ I = 0.0318 \\]\n\nThis current is the maximum current that can be measured by the galvanometer.\n\nNow, we need to find the shunt resistance required to convert the galvanometer into an ammeter capable of measuring current in the range $0-1.0 \\mathrm{~A}$. The shunt resistance \\( R_s \\) is given by the formula:\n\n\\[ R_s = \\frac{I_{max} - I_{min}}{I_{max} - I_{min}} \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( I_{max} \\) is the maximum current,\n- \\( I_{min} \\) is the minimum current.\n\nGiven values:\n- \\( I_{max} = 1.0 \\) A,\n- \\( I_{min} = 0 \\) A.\n\nPlugging these values into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[ R_s = \\frac{1.0 - 0}{1.0 - 0} \\]\n\n\\[ R_s = \\frac{1.0}{1.0} \\]\n\n\\[ R_s = 1.0 \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of the shunt resistance required to convert the galvanometer into an ammeter capable of measuring current in the range $0-1.0 \\mathrm{~A}$ is $\\boxed{1.0 \\mathrm{~ohm}}$."
