            "To solve this problem, we need to find the value of $\\ln \\left(p_{\\mathrm{H}_{2}}\\right)$ given the conditions of the problem.\n\n1. **Determine the moles of copper:**\n   The problem states that the total pressure is 1 bar, and the total moles of copper are 1 mole. Therefore, the moles of copper are 1 mole.\n\n2. **Calculate the moles of copper oxide:**\n   The reaction for the formation of copper oxide is:\n   \\[\n   2 \\mathrm{Cu}(\\mathrm{s})+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}(\\mathrm{g}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Cu}_{2} \\mathrm{O}(\\mathrm{s})\n   \\]\n   Since the total moles of copper are 1 mole, the moles of copper oxide formed will also be 1 mole.\n\n3. **Calculate the moles of water vapor:**\n   The total moles of water vapor are 1 mole.\n\n4. **Calculate the moles of $\\mathrm{H}_{2}$:**\n   The total moles of $\\mathrm{H}_{2}$ are the sum of the moles of water vapor and the moles of copper oxide:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Moles of } \\mathrm{H}_{2} = 1 \\text{ mole (water)} + 1 \\text{ mole (copper oxide)} = 2 \\text{ moles}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the partial pressure of $\\mathrm{H}_{2}$:**\n   The partial pressure of $\\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is the total pressure divided by the total moles of $\\mathrm{H}_{2}$:\n   \\[\n   p_{\\mathrm{H}_{2}} = \\frac{1 \\text{ bar}}{2 \\text{ moles}} = 0.5 \\text{ bar}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Calculate the value of $\\ln \\left(p_{\\mathrm{H}_{2}}\\right)$:**\n   Using the natural logarithm of the partial pressure of $\\mathrm{H}_{2}$:\n   \\[\n   \\ln \\left(p_{\\mathrm{H}_{2}}\\right) = \\ln (0.5)\n   \\]\n   Using the approximation $\\ln (0.5) \\approx -0.6931$, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\ln \\left(p_{\\mathrm{H}_{2}}\\right) \\approx -0.6931\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of $\\ln \\left(p_{\\mathrm{H}_{2}}\\right)$ is approximately $\\boxed{-0.6931}$."
