          "question": "Consider the following reversible reaction,\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{A}(\\mathrm{g})+\\mathrm{B}(\\mathrm{g}) \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{AB}(\\mathrm{g})\n\\]\n\nThe activation energy of the backward reaction exceeds that of the forward reaction by $2 R T$ (in $\\mathrm{J} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ ). If the pre-exponential factor of the forward reaction is 4 times that of the reverse reaction, what is the absolute value of $\\Delta G^{\\theta}$ (in $\\mathrm{J} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ ) for the reaction at $300 \\mathrm{~K}$?\n\n(Given; $\\ln (2)=0.7, R T=2500 \\mathrm{~J} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$ at $300 \\mathrm{~K}$ and $G$ is the Gibbs energy)",
          "model_outputs": [
            "To solve this problem, we need to use the Gibbs free energy equation, which is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta G^{\\theta} = \\Delta G^{\\text{forward}} - \\Delta G^{\\text{reverse}}\n\\]\n\nWe are given that the activation energy of the forward reaction is $2 R T$ and the pre-exponential factor of the forward reaction is 4 times that of the reverse reaction. We also know that the Gibbs energy of the forward reaction is $\\ln (2)$ times the Gibbs energy of the reverse reaction.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the Gibbs energy of the forward reaction:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta G^{\\text{forward}} = \\ln (2) \\times \\Delta G^{\\text{reverse}}\n\\]\n\nNow, let's calculate the Gibbs energy of the reverse reaction:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta G^{\\text{reverse}} = \\Delta G^{\\text{forward}} - \\Delta G^{\\text{forward}} = \\Delta G^{\\text{forward}} (1 - \\ln (2))\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the value of $\\Delta G^{\\text{forward}}$:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta G^{\\text{reverse}} = \\ln (2) \\times \\Delta G^{\\text{forward}} (1 - \\ln (2))\n\\]\n\nNow, let's calculate the absolute value of $\\Delta G^{\\theta}$:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta G^{\\theta} = \\Delta G^{\\text{forward}} - \\Delta G^{\\text{reverse}} = \\Delta G^{\\text{forward}} - \\Delta G^{\\text{forward}} (1 - \\ln (2)) = \\Delta G^{\\text{forward}} \\ln (2)\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the value of $\\Delta G^{\\text{forward}}$:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta G^{\\theta} = \\ln (2) \\times \\Delta G^{\\text{forward}}\n\\]\n\nNow, let's calculate the numerical value of $\\Delta G^{\\theta}$:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta G^{\\theta} = \\ln (2) \\times \\ln (2) \\times \\ln (2) = (\\ln (2))^3\n\\]\n\nUsing the approximation $\\ln (2) \\approx 0.693$:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta G^{\\theta} \\approx (0.693)^3 \\approx 0.353\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the absolute value of $\\Delta G^{\\theta}$ is approximately $\\boxed{0.35}$ $\\mathrm{J} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ .",
