          "question": "A current carrying wire heats a metal rod. The wire provides a constant power $(P)$ to the rod. The metal rod is enclosed in an insulated container. It is observed that the temperature $(T)$ in the metal rod changes with time $(t)$ as\n\n\\[\nT(t)=T_{0}\\left(1+\\beta t^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\right)\n\\]\n\nwhere $\\beta$ is a constant with appropriate dimension while $T_{0}$ is a constant with dimension of temperature. The heat capacity of the metal is,\n\n(A) $\\frac{4 P\\left(T(t)-T_{0}\\right)^{3}}{\\beta^{4} T_{0}^{4}}$\n\n(B) $\\frac{4 P\\left(T(t)-T_{0}\\right)^{4}}{\\beta^{4} T_{0}^{5}}$\n\n(C) $\\frac{4 P\\left(T(t)-T_{0}\\right)^{2}}{\\beta^{4} T_{0}^{3}}$\n\n(D) $\\frac{4 P\\left(T(t)-T_{0}\\right)}{\\beta^{4} T_{0}^{2}}$",
          "model_outputs": [
            "To solve this problem, we need to find the heat capacity of the metal rod. The heat capacity is defined as the amount of heat required to raise the temperature of a unit mass of the substance by 1 degree Celsius.\n\nGiven the temperature of the metal rod as a function of time, we can find the heat capacity by differentiating the temperature with respect to time.\n\nThe heat capacity $C$ is given by the derivative of the temperature $T$ with respect to time $t$:\n\\[\nC = \\frac{dT}{dt}\n\\]\n\nGiven the temperature function:\n\\[\nT(t) = T_0 \\left(1 + \\beta t^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\right)\n\\]\n\nWe differentiate $T(t)$ with respect to $t$:\n\\[\n\\frac{dT}{dt} = \\frac{d}{dt} \\left(T_0 \\left(1 + \\beta t^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\right)\\right)\n\\]\n\nUsing the product rule for differentiation, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{dT}{dt} = \\frac{dT_0}{dt} \\left(1 + \\beta t^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\right) + T_0 \\frac{d}{dt} \\left(1 + \\beta t^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\right)\n\\]\n\nSince $\\frac{dT_0}{dt} = 0$ (as $T_0$ is a constant), we only need to differentiate the second term:\n\\[\n\\frac{dT}{dt} = \\beta \\frac{dT_0}{dt} t^{\\frac{1}{4}} + \\frac{d}{dt} \\left(T_0\\right) + \\frac{d}{dt} \\left(\\beta t^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\right)\n\\]\n\nGiven that $\\frac{dT_0}{dt} = 0$, we simplify:\n\\[\n\\frac{dT}{dt} = \\beta \\frac{dT_0}{dt} t^{\\frac{1}{4}} + \\frac{d}{dt} \\left(T_0\\right) + \\frac{d}{dt} \\left(\\beta t^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\right)\n\\]\n\nSince $\\frac{dT_0}{dt} = 0$, we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{dT}{dt} = \\beta \\frac{dT_0}{dt} t^{\\frac{1}{4}} + \\frac{d}{dt} \\left(T_0\\right) + \\frac{d}{dt} \\left(\\beta t^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\right)\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the derivative of $T_0$ and $\\beta t^{\\frac{1}{4}}$. Since $T_0$ is a constant, its derivative is zero:\n\\[\n\\frac{dT_0}{dt} = 0\n\\]\n\nAnd for $\\beta t^{\\frac{1}{4}}$, we use the power rule:\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{dt} \\left(\\beta t^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\right) = \\beta \\frac{d}{dt} \\left(t^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\right) = \\beta \\frac{1}{4} t^{-\\frac{3}{4}}\n\\]\n\nPutting it all together:\n\\[\n\\frac{dT}{dt} = \\beta \\frac{dT_0}{dt} t^{\\frac{1}{4}} + \\frac{d}{dt} \\left(T_0\\right) + \\frac{d}{dt} \\left(\\beta t^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{dT}{dt} = \\beta \\frac{dT_0}{dt} t^{\\frac{1}{4}} + 0 + \\beta \\frac{1}{4} t^{-\\frac{3}{4}}\n\\]\n\nSince $\\frac{dT_0}{dt} = 0$, we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{dT}{dt} = \\beta \\frac{1}{4} t^{-\\frac{3}{4}}\n\\]\n\nNow, we can find the heat capacity $C$ by multiplying the heat capacity of the metal by the temperature difference:\n\\[\nC = \\frac{dT}{dt} = \\beta \\frac{1}{4} t^{-\\frac{3}{4}}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{C}\n\\]",
