          "type": "MCQ(multiple)",
          "question": "Let\n\n\\[\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{lll}\n\n0 & 1 & a \\\\\n\n1 & 2 & 3 \\\\\n\n3 & b & 1\n\n\\end{array}\\right] \\quad \\text { and adj } M=\\left[\\begin{array}{rrr}\n\n-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\\n\n8 & -6 & 2 \\\\\n\n-5 & 3 & -1\n\n\\end{array}\\right]\n\\]\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are real numbers. Which of the following options is/are correct?\n\n(A) $a+b=3$\n\n(B) $(\\operatorname{adj} M)^{-1}+\\operatorname{adj} M^{-1}=-M$\n\n(C) $\\operatorname{det}\\left(\\operatorname{adj} M^{2}\\right)=81$\n\n(D) If $M\\left[\\begin{array}{l}\\alpha \\\\ \\beta \\\\ \\gamma\\end{array}\\right]=\\left[\\begin{array}{l}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{array}\\right]$, then $\\alpha-\\beta+\\gamma=3$",
          "model_outputs": [
            "To solve this problem, we will examine each option step by step.\n\n**Option (A):**\n\nWe start by calculating the determinant of the matrix $M$:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{det}(M) = \\begin{vmatrix}\n0 & 1 & a \\\\\n1 & 2 & 3 \\\\\n3 & b & 1\n\\end{vmatrix}\n= 0 \\cdot \\begin{vmatrix} 2 & 3 \\\\ b & 1 \\end{vmatrix} - 1 \\cdot \\begin{vmatrix} 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\end{vmatrix} + a \\cdot \\begin{vmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & b \\end{vmatrix}\n\\]\n\\[\n= 0 - 1(1 - 9) + a(b - 6)\n\\]\n\\[\n= -8 + ab - 6a\n\\]\n\\[\n= ab - 6a - 8\n\\]\n\nFor $a+b=3$, we have:\n\\[\na + b = 3 \\implies ab - 6a - 8 = 0 \\implies ab - 6a = 8 \\implies a(b - 6) = 8\n\\]\n\nThis does not necessarily imply $a+b=3$. Therefore, option (A) is incorrect.\n\n**Option (B):**\n\nWe need to check if $(\\operatorname{adj} M)^{-1}+\\operatorname{adj} M^{-1}=-M$.\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of $\\operatorname{adj} M$:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{adj} M = \\begin{pmatrix}\n-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\\n8 & -6 & 2 \\\\\n-5 & 3 & -1\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\operatorname{adj} M^{-1} = \\frac{1}{\\det(\\operatorname{adj} M)} \\begin{pmatrix}\n-6 & -1 & 1 \\\\\n8 & -1 & -2 \\\\\n5 & -3 & 1\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\\[\n= \\frac{1}{(-1)(-1)(-1-8-6)} \\begin{pmatrix}\n-6 & -1 & 1 \\\\\n8 & -1 & -2 \\\\\n5 & -3 & 1\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\\[\n= \\frac{1}{27} \\begin{pmatrix}\n-6 & -1 & 1 \\\\\n8 & -1 & -2 \\\\\n5 & -3 & 1\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nNow, we add $\\operatorname{adj} M^{-1}$ and $\\operatorname{adj} M^{-1}$:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{adj} M^{-1}+\\operatorname{adj} M^{-1} = \\frac{1}{27} \\begin{pmatrix}\n-6 & -1 & 1 \\\\\n8 & -1 & -2 \\\\\n5 & -3 & 1\n\\end{pmatrix} + \\frac{1}{27} \\begin{pmatrix}\n-6 & -1 & 1 \\\\\n8 & -1 & -2 \\\\\n5 & -3 & 1\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\\[\n= \\frac{1}{27} \\begin{pmatrix}\n-6 & -1 & 1 \\\\\n8 & -1 & -2 \\\\\n5 & -3 & 1\n\\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix}\n1 & 0 & 0 \\\\\n1 & 1 & 0 \\\\\n1 & 1 & 1\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\\[\n= \\frac{1}{27} \\begin{pmatrix}\n-6 + 8 - 5 & -1 + (-1) + 0 & 1 \\\\\n8 - 8 - 2 & -1 + (-1) + 0 & -2 \\\\\n5 - 3 + 1 & -3 + (-3) + 0 & 1\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\\[\n= \\frac{1}{27} \\begin{pmatrix}\n-3 & -2 & 1 \\\\\n-2 & -4 & -2 \\\\\n3 & -6 & 1\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThis is not equal to $-M$, so option (B) is incorrect.\n\n**Option (C):**\n\nWe calculate the determinant of $(\\operatorname{adj} M)^2$:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{det}(\\operatorname{adj} M^2) = \\begin{vmatrix}\n-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\\n8 & -6 & 2 \\\\\n-5 & 3 & -1\n\\end{vmatrix}\n\\begin{vmatrix}\n-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\\n8 & -6 & 2 \\\\\n-5 & 3 & -1\n\\end{vmatrix}\n\\]\n\\[\n= (-1)(-1)(-1-8-6)(-1)(-1)(-1-8-6)\n\\]\n\\[\n= (-1)(-1)(-15)(-1)(-1)(-15)\n\\]\n\\[\n= (-1)(-1)(15)(15)\n\\]\n\\[\n= 225\n\\]\n\nThis is not equal to $81$, so option (C) is incorrect.\n\n**Option (D):**\n\nWe need to check if $\\alpha-\\beta+\\gamma=3$ if $M\\left[\\begin{array}{l}\\alpha \\\\ \\beta \\\\ \\gamma\\end{array}\\right]=\\left[\\begin{array}{l}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{array}\\right]$.\n\nFrom the given matrix $M$, we have:\n\\[\nM\\left[\\begin{array}{l}\\alpha \\\\ \\beta \\\\ \\gamma\\end{array}\\right] = \\begin{pmatrix}\n0 & 1 & a \\\\\n1 & 2 & 3 \\\\\n3 & b & 1\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n\\alpha \\\\ \\beta \\\\ \\gamma\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\\[\n= \\begin{pmatrix}\n0\\alpha + 1\\beta + a\\gamma \\\\\n1\\alpha + 2\\beta + 3\\gamma \\\\\n3\\alpha + b\\beta + \\gamma\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\\[\n= \\begin{pmatrix}\n\\beta + a\\gamma \\\\\n\\alpha + 2\\beta + 3\\gamma \\\\\n3\\alpha + b\\beta + \\gamma\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nGiven that $\\alpha-\\beta+\\gamma=3$, we can rewrite the matrix as:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n\\beta + a\\gamma \\\\\n\\alpha + 2\\beta + 3\\gamma \\\\\n3\\alpha + b\\beta + \\gamma\n\\end{pmatrix}\n= \\begin{pmatrix}\n\\beta + a\\gamma \\\\\n\\alpha + 2\\beta + 3\\gamma \\\\\n3\\alpha + \\beta + b\\gamma\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nComparing the corresponding entries, we get:\n\\[\n\\beta + a\\gamma = \\alpha + 2\\beta + 3\\gamma\n\\]\n\\[\n\\alpha + 2\\beta + 3\\gamma = 3\\alpha + \\beta + b\\gamma\n\\]\n\nFrom the first equation, we have:\n\\[\n\\beta + a\\gamma = \\alpha + 2\\beta + 3\\gamma \\implies a\\gamma = \\alpha + \\beta + 3\\gamma\n\\]\n\nFrom the second equation, we have:\n\\[\n\\alpha + 2\\beta + 3\\gamma = 3\\alpha + \\beta + b\\gamma \\implies 2\\beta + 3\\gamma = 2\\alpha + b\\gamma\n\\]\n\nSince $\\alpha-\\beta+\\gamma=3$, we can substitute $\\alpha = \\beta + \\gamma + 3$ into the equations:\n\\[\na\\gamma = (\\beta + \\gamma + 3) + \\beta + 3\\gamma = 2\\beta + 4\\gamma + 3\n\\]\n\\[\n2\\beta + 3\\gamma = 2(\\beta + \\gamma + 3) + b\\gamma = 2\\beta + 2\\gamma + 6 + b\\gamma\n\\]\n\nSolving these equations, we find that $a=2$ and $b=3$. Therefore, $\\alpha-\\beta+\\gamma=3$ is correct.\n\nThus, the correct option is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{D}\n\\]",
