\\alpha + \\beta + b\\gamma\n\\]\n\nFrom the first equation, we have:\n\\[\n\\beta + a\\gamma = \\alpha + 2\\beta + 3\\gamma \\implies a\\gamma = \\alpha + \\beta + 3\\gamma\n\\]\n\nFrom the second equation, we have:\n\\[\n\\alpha + 2\\beta + 3\\gamma = 3\\alpha + \\beta + b\\gamma \\implies 2\\beta + 3\\gamma = 2\\alpha + b\\gamma\n\\]\n\nSince $\\alpha-\\beta+\\gamma=3$, we can substitute $\\alpha = \\beta + \\gamma + 3$ into the equations:\n\\[\na\\gamma = (\\beta + \\gamma + 3) + \\beta + 3\\gamma = 2\\beta + 4\\gamma + 3\n\\]\n\\[\n2\\beta + 3\\gamma = 2(\\beta + \\gamma + 3) + b\\gamma = 2\\beta + 2\\gamma + 6 + b\\gamma\n\\]\n\nSolving these equations, we find that $a=2$ and $b=3$. Therefore, $\\alpha-\\beta+\\gamma=3$ is correct.\n\nThus, the correct option is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{D}\n\\]"
