          "model_outputs": [
            "\n1. **Understanding the Problem:**\n   - We have an optical bench with a scale that has four equal divisions in each $\\mathrm{cm}$.\n   - The scale is $1.5 \\mathrm{~m}$ long.\n   - The focal length of a convex lens is measured at $75 \\mathrm{~cm}$ mark.\n   - The object pin is kept at $45 \\mathrm{~cm}$ mark.\n   - The image of the object pin on the other side of the lens overlaps with the image pin that is kept at $135 \\mathrm{~cm}$ mark.\n\n2. **Calculating the Focal Length:**\n   - The scale is $1.5 \\mathrm{~m}$ long, which is equivalent to $1.5 \\times 100 \\mathrm{~cm}$.\n   - The object pin is at $45 \\mathrm{~cm}$ mark, and the image pin is at $135 \\mathrm{~cm}$ mark.\n   - The distance between the object pin and the image pin is $135 \\mathrm{~cm} - 45 \\mathrm{~cm} = 90 \\mathrm{~cm}$.\n   - Since the scale has four equal divisions in each $\\mathrm{cm}$, the distance between two consecutive divisions is $\\frac{1.5 \\mathrm{~m}}{4} = 0.375 \\mathrm{~m}$.\n   - The distance between the object pin and the first division is $45 \\mathrm{~cm} \\times \\frac{1}{4} = 11.25 \\mathrm{~cm}$.\n   - The distance between the first division and the image pin is $90 \\mathrm{~cm} - 11.25 \\mathrm{~cm} = 78.75 \\mathrm{~cm}$.\n   - The distance between the second division and the image pin is $78.75 \\mathrm{~cm} \\times \\frac{1}{4} = 19.6875 \\mathrm{~cm}$.\n   - The distance between the second division and the image pin is $90 \\mathrm{~cm} - 19.6875 \\mathrm{~cm} = 70.3125 \\mathrm{~cm}$.\n   - The distance between the third division and the image pin is $70.3125 \\mathrm{~cm} \\times \\frac{1}{4} = 17.78125 \\mathrm{~cm}$.\n   - The distance between the third division and the image pin is $90 \\mathrm{~cm} - 17.78125 \\mathrm{~cm} = 72.21875 \\mathrm{~cm}$.\n   - The distance between the fourth division and the image pin is $72.21875 \\mathrm{~cm} \\times \\frac{1}{4} = 18.0559375 \\mathrm{~cm}$.\n   - The distance between the fourth division and the image pin is $90 \\mathrm{~cm} - 18.0559375 \\mathrm{~cm} = 71.9440625 \\mathrm{~cm}$.\n   - The total distance from the object pin to the image pin is $90 \\mathrm{~cm} - 71.9440625 \\mathrm{~cm} = 18.0559375 \\mathrm{~cm}$.\n   - The focal length of the lens is the total distance from the object pin to the image pin divided by the distance between the object pin and the first division, which is $18.0559375 \\mathrm{~cm} / 11.25 \\mathrm{~cm} = 1.6 \\mathrm{~m}$.\n\n3. **Calculating the Percentage Error:**\n   - The measured focal length is $1.6 \\mathrm{~m}$.\n   - The true focal length is $1.5 \\mathrm{~m}$.\n   - The percentage error is calculated as:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Percentage Error} = \\left( \\frac{\\text{Measured Focal Length} - \\text{True Focal Length}}{\\text{True Focal Length}} \\right) \\times 100\\%\n     \\]\n   - Substituting the values:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Percentage Error} = \\left( \\frac{1.6 \\mathrm{~m} - 1.5 \\mathrm{~m}}{1.5 \\mathrm{~m}} \\right) \\times 100\\% = \\left( \\frac{0.1 \\mathrm{~m}}{1.5 \\mathrm{~m}} \\right) \\times 100\\% = \\frac{0.1}{1.5} \\times 100\\% = 6.67\\%\n     \\]\n\n4. **Conclusion:**\n   - The percentage error in the measurement of the focal length of the lens is $6.67\\%$.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6.67\\%}\n\\]",
