          "question": "A stationary tuning fork is in resonance with an air column in a pipe. If the tuning fork is moved with a speed of $2 \\mathrm{~ms}^{-1}$ in front of the open end of the pipe and parallel to it, the length of the pipe should be changed for the resonance to occur with the moving tuning fork. If the speed of sound in air is $320 \\mathrm{~ms}^{-1}$, what is the smallest value of the percentage change required in the length of the pipe?",
          "model_outputs": [
            "\n1. **Understanding the Problem:**\n   - We have a stationary tuning fork in resonance with an air column in a pipe.\n   - The tuning fork is moved with a speed of $2 \\mathrm{~ms}^{-1}$ in front of the open end of the pipe and parallel to it.\n   - We need to find the smallest value of the percentage change required in the length of the pipe for the resonance to occur.\n\n2. **Setting Up the Problem:**\n   - Let the initial length of the pipe be $L_0$.\n   - The speed of sound in air is $v_s = 320 \\mathrm{~ms}^{-1}$.\n   - The speed of the tuning fork is $v_f = 2 \\mathrm{~ms}^{-1}$.\n   - The frequency of the tuning fork is $f_f$.\n\n3. **Using the Resonance Condition:**\n   - The resonance condition for a stationary tuning fork in a pipe is given by:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{v_s}{v_f} = \\frac{L_0}{L}\n     \\]\n   - Here, $v_s$ is the speed of sound in air, $v_f$ is the speed of the tuning fork, and $L_0$ is the initial length of the pipe.\n\n4. **Substituting the Values:**\n   - Substitute the given values into the resonance condition:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{320}{2} = \\frac{L_0}{L}\n     \\]\n   - Simplify the equation:\n     \\[\n     160 = \\frac{L_0}{L}\n     \\]\n\n5. **Finding the New Length:**\n   - To find the new length $L$ for resonance, we rearrange the equation:\n     \\[\n     L = \\frac{L_0}{160}\n     \\]\n\n6. **Percentage Change in Length:**\n   - The percentage change in length is given by:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Percentage Change} = \\left(\\frac{L_0 - L}{L_0}\\right) \\times 100\\%\n     \\]\n   - Substitute the values:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Percentage Change} = \\left(\\frac{L_0 - \\frac{L_0}{160}}{L_0}\\right) \\times 100\\%\n     \\]\n   - Simplify the expression:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Percentage Change} = \\left(\\frac{160 - 1}{160}\\right) \\times 100\\%\n     \\]\n   - Calculate the percentage change:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Percentage Change} = \\left(\\frac{159}{160}\\right) \\times 100\\%\n     \\]\n   - Perform the di