he temperature of the ideal gas:**\n   The change in the temperature of the ideal gas is given by:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta T = \\frac{1.23 \\mathrm{~V} \\cdot \\frac{1.00 \\mathrm{~mol}}{1.00 \\mathrm{~mol}} \\cdot 8.314 \\mathrm{~J} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \\mathrm{~K}^{-1}}{96500 \\mathrm{C} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying the expression:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta T = \\frac{1.23 \\mathrm{~V} \\cdot 8.314 \\mathrm{~J} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \\mathrm{~K}^{-1}}{96500 \\mathrm{C} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\Delta T = \\frac{1.23 \\cdot 8.314}{96500} \\mathrm{~K}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\Delta T = \\frac{10.4}{96500} \\mathrm{~K}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\Delta T = 0.000108 \\mathrm{~K}\n   \\]\n\n10. **Final answer:**\n    The change in the temperature of the ideal gas is $\\boxed{0.000108 \\mathrm{~K}}$.",
