          "type": "MCQ(multiple)",
          "question": "Let $x, y$ and $z$ be positive real numbers. Suppose $x, y$ and $z$ are the lengths of the sides of a triangle opposite to its angles $X, Y$ and $Z$, respectively. If\n\n\\[\n\\tan \\frac{X}{2}+\\tan \\frac{Z}{2}=\\frac{2 y}{x+y+z}\n\\]\n\nthen which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\n\n(A) $2 Y=X+Z$\n\n(B) $Y=X+Z$\n\n(C) $\\tan \\frac{x}{2}=\\frac{x}{y+z}$\n\n(D) $x^{2}+z^{2}-y^{2}=x z$",
          "model_outputs": [
            "\n1. **Given Information:**\n   We are given that \\( x, y, z \\) are the sides of a triangle opposite to its angles \\( X, Y, Z \\) respectively. We are also given the equation:\n   \\[\n   \\tan \\frac{X}{2} + \\tan \\frac{Z}{2} = \\frac{2y}{x+y+z}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Using the Tangent Half-Angle Formulas:**\n   Recall the tangent half-angle formulas for a triangle:\n   \\[\n   \\tan \\frac{X}{2} = \\frac{r}{s-x}, \\quad \\tan \\frac{Z}{2} = \\frac{r}{s-z}\n   \\]\n   where \\( r \\) is the inradius and \\( s \\) is the semiperimeter of the triangle.\n\n3. **Substituting the Tangent Half-Angle Formulas:**\n   Substitute the tangent half-angle formulas into the given equation:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{r}{s-x} + \\frac{r}{s-z} = \\frac{2y}{x+y+z}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Simplifying the Equation:**\n   Combine the fractions on the left-hand side:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{r(s-z) + r(s-x)}{(s-x)(s-z)} = \\frac{2y}{x+y+z}\n   \\]\n   Simplify the numerator:\n   \\[\n   r(2s - x - z) = \\frac{2y(s-x)(s-z)}{x+y+z}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Using the Semiperimeter \\( s \\):**\n   Recall that \\( s = \\frac{x+y+z}{2} \\). Substitute \\( s \\) into the equation:\n   \\[\n   r(2 \\cdot \\frac{x+y+z}{2} - x - z) = \\frac{2y(s-x)(s-z)}{x+y+z}\n   \\]\n   Simplify the left-hand side:\n   \\[\n   r(x + y + z - x - z) = r(y)\n   \\]\n   So, we have:\n   \\[\n   r(y) = \\frac{2y(s-x)(s-z)}{x+y+z}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Simplifying the Right-Hand Side:**\n   Recall that \\( s-x = \\frac{y+z}{2} \\) and \\( s-z = \\frac{x+y}{2} \\). Substitute these into the right-hand side:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2y(s-x)(s-z)}{x+y+z} = \\frac{2y \\left(\\frac{y+z}{2}\\right) \\left(\\frac{x+y}{2}\\right)}{\\frac{x+y+z}{2}} = \\frac{y(y+z)(x+y)}{x+y+z} = y(y+z)\n   \\]\n\n7. **Equating the Two Expressions:**\n   Since \\( r(y) = y(y+z) \\), we have:\n   \\[\n   r = y + z\n   \\]\n\n8. **Using the Inradius \\( r \\):**\n   Recall that \\( r = \\frac{A}{s} \\), where \\( A \\) is the area of the triangle. Substitute \\( r = y + z \\) into the area formula:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{A}{s} = y + z\n   \\]\n   Since \\( s = \\frac{x+y+z}{2} \\), we have:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{A}{\\frac{x+y+z}{2}} = y + z\n   \\]\n   Simplify:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2A}{x+y+z} = y + z\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   A = \\frac{(y+z)(x+y+z)}{2}\n   \\]\n\n9. **Using the Area Formula:**\n   Recall the area formula for a triangle:\n   \\[\n   A = \\sqrt{s(s-x)(s-y)(s-z)}\n   \\]\n   Substitute \\( A = \\frac{(y+z)(x+y+z)}{2} \\) and \\( s = \\frac{x+y+z}{2} \\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(y+z)(x+y+z)}{2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{x+y+z}{2} \\left(\\frac{x+y+z}{2} - x\\right) \\left(\\frac{x+y+z}{2} - y\\right) \\left(\\frac{x+y+z}{2} - z\\right)}\n   \\]\n   Simplify the right-hand side:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(y+z)(x+y+z)}{2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{x+y+z}{2} \\left(\\frac{y+z}{2}\\right) \\left(\\frac{x+y}{2}\\right) \\left(\\frac{x+y+z}{2}\\right)}\n   \\]\n   Simplify further:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(y+z)(x+y+z)}{2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{(x+y+z)^2}{16}}\n   \\]\n   Simplify the square root:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(y+z)(x+y+z)}{2} = \\frac{x+y+z}{4}\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   (y+z)(x+y+z) = 2(x+y+z)\n   \\]\n   Since \\( x, y, z \\) are positive real numbers, we can divide both sides by \\( x+y+z \\):\n   \\[\n   y+z = 2\n   \\]\n\n10. **Conclusion:**\n    Since \\( y+z = 2 \\), we have:\n    \\[\n    2Y = X + Z\n    \\]\n\nThus, the correct answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{A}\n\\]",
