) into the area formula:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{A}{s} = y + z\n   \\]\n   Since \\( s = \\frac{x+y+z}{2} \\), we have:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{A}{\\frac{x+y+z}{2}} = y + z\n   \\]\n   Simplify:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2A}{x+y+z} = y + z\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   A = \\frac{(y+z)(x+y+z)}{2}\n   \\]\n\n9. **Using the Area Formula:**\n   Recall the area formula for a triangle:\n   \\[\n   A = \\sqrt{s(s-x)(s-y)(s-z)}\n   \\]\n   Substitute \\( A = \\frac{(y+z)(x+y+z)}{2} \\) and \\( s = \\frac{x+y+z}{2} \\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(y+z)(x+y+z)}{2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{x+y+z}{2} \\left(\\frac{x+y+z}{2} - x\\right) \\left(\\frac{x+y+z}{2} - y\\right) \\left(\\frac{x+y+z}{2} - z\\right)}\n   \\]\n   Simplify the right-hand side:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(y+z)(x+y+z)}{2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{x+y+z}{2} \\left(\\frac{y+z}{2}\\right) \\left(\\frac{x+y}{2}\\right) \\left(\\frac{x+y+z}{2}\\right)}\n   \\]\n   Simplify further:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(y+z)(x+y+z)}{2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{(x+y+z)^2}{16}}\n   \\]\n   Simplify the square root:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(y+z)(x+y+z)}{2} = \\frac{x+y+z}{4}\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   (y+z)(x+y+z) = 2(x+y+z)\n   \\]\n   Since \\( x, y, z \\) are positive real numbers, we can divide both sides by \\( x+y+z \\):\n   \\[\n   y+z = 2\n   \\]\n\n10. **Conclusion:**\n    Since \\( y+z = 2 \\), we have:\n    \\[\n    2Y = X + Z\n    \\]\n\nThus, the correct answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{A}\n\\]",
