            "\n1. **Define the variables and given information:**\n   - Let the molarity of silver nitrate solution $\\mathbf{A}$ be $x$ M.\n   - The molarity of barium chloride solution $\\mathbf{B}$ is $x$ M as well.\n   - The volume of solution $\\mathbf{A}$ is $V_A$ liters.\n   - The volume of solution $\\mathbf{B}$ is $V_B$ liters.\n   - The boiling point of pure water is $100^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$.\n\n2. **Calculate the number of moles of each solute:**\n   - Moles of silver nitrate in solution $\\mathbf{A}$: $x \\times V_A$\n   - Moles of barium chloride in solution $\\mathbf{B}$: $x \\times V_B$\n\n3. **Calculate the total volume of the solution:**\n   - Total volume of solution $\\mathbf{A}$: $V_A + V_B$\n\n4. **Calculate the molarity of the solution $\\mathbf{B}$:**\n   - Moles of silver nitrate in solution $\\mathbf{B}$: $x \\times V_B$\n   - Moles of barium chloride in solution $\\mathbf{B}$: $x \\times V_B$\n   - Total volume of solution $\\mathbf{B}$: $V_A + V_B$\n   - Molarity of solution $\\mathbf{B}$: $\\frac{x \\times V_B}{V_A + V_B}$\n\n5. **Calculate the boiling point of solution $\\mathbf{B}$:**\n   - Using the boiling point of pure water and the molarity of solution $\\mathbf{B}$:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Boiling point of solution } \\mathbf{B} = 100^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} + \\frac{x \\times V_B}{V_A + V_B} \\times 0.5 \\mathrm{~K} \\mathrm{~kg} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Calculate the boiling point of solution $\\mathbf{A}$:**\n   - Using the boiling point of pure water and the molarity of solution $\\mathbf{A}$:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Boiling point of solution } \\mathbf{A} = 100^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} + \\frac{x \\times V_A}{V_A + V_B} \\times 0.5 \\mathrm{~K} \\mathrm{~kg} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Calculate the difference in boiling points:**\n   - The difference in boiling points is:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta T = \\left(100^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} + \\frac{x \\times V_B}{V_A + V_B} \\times 0.5 \\mathrm{~K} \\mathrm{~kg} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}\\right) - \\left(100^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} + \\frac{x \\times V_A}{V_A + V_B} \\times 0.5 \\mathrm{~K} \\mathrm{~kg} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}\\right)\n   \\]\n   - Simplifying the difference:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta T = \\frac{x \\times V_B}{V_A + V_B} \\times 0.5 \\mathrm{~K} \\mathrm{~kg} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1} - \\frac{x \\times V_A}{V_A + V_B} \\times 0.5 \\mathrm{~K} \\mathrm{~kg} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}\n   \\]\n   - Factoring out the common terms:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta T = 0.5 \\mathrm{~K} \\mathrm{~kg} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \\left(\\frac{x \\times V_B}{V_A + V_B} - \\frac{x \\times V_A}{V_A + V_B}\\right)\n   \\]\n   - Simplifying the expression inside the parentheses:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta T = 0.5 \\mathrm{~K} \\mathrm{~kg} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \\left(\\frac{x \\times (V_B - V_A)}{V_A + V_B}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n8. **Calculate the molarity of solution $\\mathbf{A}$:**\n   - Using the boiling point of solution $\\mathbf{A}$ and the molarity of solution $\\mathbf{A}$:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{\\Delta T}{0.5 \\mathrm{~K} \\mathrm{~kg} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \\left(\\frac{x \\times (V_B - V_A)}{V_A + V_B}\\right)}\n   \\]\n\n9. **Calculate the value of $|\\mathbf{y}|$:**\n   - The value of $|\\mathbf{y}|$ is the absolute value of the difference in boiling points:\n   \\[\n   |\\mathbf{y}| = \\left|\\frac{x \\times (V_B - V_A)}{V_A + V_B}\\right|\n   \\]\n\n10. **Final answer:**\n    - The value of $|\\mathbf{y}|$ is:\n    \\[\n    |\\mathbf{y}| = \\boxed{0.5 \\times 10^{-2} \\mathrm{~K} \\mathrm{~kg} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}\n    \\]",
