          "model_outputs": [
            "\n1. **Rewrite the equation**:\n   We start by rewriting the given equation:\n   \\[\n   3x^2 - 4|x^2 - 1| + x - 1 = 0\n   \\]\n   This can be split into two cases based on the absolute value:\n   \\[\n   3x^2 - 4(x^2 - 1) + x - 1 = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad 3x^2 - 4(-x^2 + 1) + x - 1 = 0\n   \\]\n\n2. **Case 1: \\( x^2 \\geq 1 \\)**\n   In this case, \\( |x^2 - 1| = x^2 - 1 \\). Substituting this into the equation, we get:\n   \\[\n   3x^2 - 4(x^2 - 1) + x - 1 = 0\n   \\]\n   Simplifying this, we have:\n   \\[\n   3x^2 - 4x^2 + 4 + x - 1 = 0 \\implies -x^2 + x + 3 = 0\n   \\]\n   Multiplying through by -1, we get:\n   \\[\n   x^2 - x - 3 = 0\n   \\]\n   Factoring the quadratic equation, we find:\n   \\[\n   (x - 3)(x + 1) = 0\n   \\]\n   This gives us two solutions:\n   \\[\n   x = 3 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = -1\n   \\]\n   Since \\( x^2 \\geq 1 \\), we discard \\( x = -1 \\) as it does not satisfy this condition.\n\n3. **Case 2: \\( x^2 < 1 \\)**\n   In this case, \\( |x^2 - 1| = -(x^2 - 1) \\). Substituting this into the equation, we get:\n   \\[\n   3x^2 - 4(-x^2 + 1) + x - 1 = 0\n   \\]\n   Simplifying this, we have:\n   \\[\n   3x^2 + 4x^2 - 4 + x - 1 = 0 \\implies 7x^2 + x - 5 = 0\n   \\]\n   This is a quadratic equation in the form \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\). We can solve it using the quadratic formula:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\n   \\]\n   Here, \\( a = 7 \\), \\( b = 1 \\), and \\( c = -5 \\). Plugging these values into the formula, we get:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1^2 - 4 \\cdot 7 \\cdot (-5)}}{2 \\cdot 7} = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 + 140}}{14} = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{141}}{14}\n   \\]\n   Since \\( x^2 < 1 \\), we discard the negative solution:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{-1 + \\sqrt{141}}{14}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Conclusion**:\n   The solutions to the equation are \\( x = 3 \\) and \\( x = \\frac{-1 + \\sqrt{141}}{14} \\).\n\nTherefore, the number of real roots of the equation is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
