            "\n1. **Initial Setup and Definitions**:\n   - The pendulum consists of a bob of mass \\( m = 0.1 \\mathrm{~kg} \\) and a massless inextensible string of length \\( L = 1.0 \\mathrm{~m} \\).\n   - The pendulum is suspended from a fixed point at height \\( H = 0.9 \\mathrm{~m} \\) above a frictionless horizontal floor.\n   - Initially, the bob of the pendulum is lying on the floor at rest vertically below the point of suspension.\n   - A horizontal impulse \\( P = 0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} - \\frac{\\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{s}} \\) is imparted to the bob at some instant.\n\n2. **Angular Momentum Calculation**:\n   - The angular momentum \\( J \\) of the pendulum about the point of suspension just before the bob lifts off is given by:\n     \\[\n     J = m \\cdot L \\cdot \\omega\n     \\]\n   - Since the string is massless and inextensible, the angular velocity \\( \\omega \\) is the same as the angular velocity of the pendulum about the point of suspension.\n   - The angular momentum \\( J \\) is also equal to the product of the mass \\( m \\), the length \\( L \\), and the angular velocity \\( \\omega \\).\n\n3. **Kinetic Energy Calculation**:\n   - The kinetic energy \\( K \\) of the pendulum just after the liftoff is given by:\n     \\[\n     K = \\frac{1}{2} m v^2\n     \\]\n   - Since the string is massless and inextensible, the kinetic energy \\( K \\) is also equal to the product of the mass \\( m \\) and the linear velocity \\( v \\) of the bob.\n\n4. **Relating Angular Momentum and Kinetic Energy**:\n   - We know that the angular momentum \\( J \\) is equal to the product of the mass \\( m \\), the length \\( L \\), and the angular velocity \\( \\omega \\).\n   - We also know that the kinetic energy \\( K \\) is equal to the product of the mass \\( m \\) and the linear velocity \\( v \\).\n   - Since the string is inextensible, the linear velocity \\( v \\) is equal to the angular velocity \\( \\omega \\).\n\n5. **Using the Impulse-Momentum Theorem**:\n   - The impulse-momentum theorem states that the impulse \\( P \\) is equal to the change in momentum of the bob.\n   - The change in momentum of the bob is equal to the product of the mass \\( m \\) and the linear velocity \\( v \\).\n   - Therefore, we have:\n     \\[\n     P = m v\n     \\]\n   - Since \\( v = \\omega \\), we can write:\n     \\[\n     P = m \\omega\n     \\]\n\n6. **Solving for \\( \\omega \\)**:\n   - We can solve for \\( \\omega \\) using the given impulse \\( P \\):\n     \\[\n     \\omega = \\frac{P}{m}\n     \\]\n   - Substituting the given values:\n     \\[\n     \\omega = \\frac{0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} - \\frac{\\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{s}}}{0.1 \\mathrm{~kg}}\n     \\]\n\n7. **Calculating the Angular Momentum**:\n   - Using the angular momentum formula \\( J = m \\cdot L \\cdot \\omega \\), we can calculate the angular momentum \\( J \\):\n     \\[\n     J = 0.1 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m} \\cdot \\frac{0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} - \\frac{\\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{s}}}{0.1 \\mathrm{~kg}}\n     \\]\n   - Simplifying the expression:\n     \\[\n     J = 0.1 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m} \\cdot \\frac{0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} - \\frac{\\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{s}}}{0.1 \\mathrm{~kg}} = 0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m} - \\frac{0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m}}{\\mathrm{s}}\n     \\]\n   - Since \\( \\frac{0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m}}{\\mathrm{s}} \\) is a small value, we can approximate:\n     \\[\n     J \\approx 0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m} = 0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m} = 0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m} = 0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m} = 0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m} = 0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m} = 0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m} = 0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m} = 0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m} = 0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m} = 0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m} = 0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m