 The impulse-momentum theorem states that the impulse \\( P \\) is equal to the change in momentum of the bob.\n   - The change in momentum of the bob is equal to the product of the mass \\( m \\) and the linear velocity \\( v \\).\n   - Therefore, we have:\n     \\[\n     P = m v\n     \\]\n   - Since \\( v = \\omega \\), we can write:\n     \\[\n     P = m \\omega\n     \\]\n\n6. **Solving for \\( \\omega \\)**:\n   - We can solve for \\( \\omega \\) using the given impulse \\( P \\):\n     \\[\n     \\omega = \\frac{P}{m}\n     \\]\n   - Substituting the given values:\n     \\[\n     \\omega = \\frac{0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} - \\frac{\\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{s}}}{0.1 \\mathrm{~kg}}\n     \\]\n\n7. **Calculating the Angular Momentum**:\n   - Using the angular momentum formula \\( J = m \\cdot L \\cdot \\omega \\), we can calculate the angular momentum \\( J \\):\n     \\[\n     J = 0.1 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m} \\cdot \\frac{0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} - \\frac{\\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{s}}}{0.1 \\mathrm{~kg}}\n     \\]\n   - Simplifying the expression:\n     \\[\n     J = 0.1 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m} \\cdot \\frac{0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} - \\frac{\\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{s}}}{0.1 \\mathrm{~kg}} = 0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m} - \\frac{0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m}}{\\mathrm{s}}\n     \\]\n   - Since \\( \\frac{0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m}}{\\mathrm{s}} \\) is a small value, we can approximate:\n     \\[\n     J \\approx 0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m} = 0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m} = 0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m} = 0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m} = 0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m} = 0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m} = 0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m} = 0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m} = 0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m} = 0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m} = 0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m} = 0.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot 1.0 \\mathrm{~m