          "type": "Integer",
          "question": "Consider a helium (He) atom that absorbs a photon of wavelength $330 \\mathrm{~nm}$. What is the change in the velocity (in $\\mathrm{cm} \\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ ) of He atom after the photon absorption?\n\n(Assume: Momentum is conserved when photon is absorbed.\n\nUse: Planck constant $=6.6 \\times 10^{-34} \\mathrm{~J} \\mathrm{~s}$, Avogadro number $=6 \\times 10^{23} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$, Molar mass of $\\mathrm{He}=4 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$ )",
          "model_outputs": [
            "To solve this problem, we need to use the relationship between the energy of a photon and the change in the velocity of an object. The energy of a photon is given by the formula $E = h\\nu$, where $h$ is the Planck constant and $\\nu$ is the frequency of the photon.\n\nGiven:\n- Wavelength of the photon, $\\lambda = 330 \\mathrm{~nm} = 330 \\times 10^{-9} \\mathrm{~m}$\n- Planck constant, $h = 6.6 \\times 10^{-34} \\mathrm{~J} \\mathrm{~s}$\n- Avogadro number, $N_A = 6 \\times 10^{23} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$\n- Molar mass of helium, $M = 4 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1} = 0.004 \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$\n\nFirst, we calculate the energy of the photon:\n\\[ E = h\\nu = (6.6 \\times 10^{-34} \\mathrm{~J} \\mathrm{~s})(330 \\times 10^{-9} \\mathrm{~m}) = 2.178 \\times 10^{-19} \\mathrm{~J} \\mathrm{~m}^{-1} \\]\n\nNext, we convert the energy of the photon to the energy of the helium atom:\n\\[ E_{\\text{He}} = \\frac{E}{M} = \\frac{2.178 \\times 10^{-19} \\mathrm{~J} \\mathrm{~m}^{-1}}{0.004 \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{~g}^{-1}} = 5.445 \\times 10^{-16} \\mathrm{~J} \\mathrm{~m}^{-1} \\]\n\nNow, we use the relationship between the energy of a photon and the change in the velocity of an object:\n\\[ E = h\\nu = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2 \\]\nwhere $v$ is the change in velocity.\n\nRearranging the equation to solve for $v^2$:\n\\[ v^2 = \\frac{2E}{h} \\]\n\nSubstituting the values of $E$ and $h$:\n\\[ v^2 = \\frac{2(5.445 \\times 10^{-16} \\mathrm{~J} \\mathrm{~m}^{-1})}{6.6 \\times 10^{-34} \\mathrm{~J} \\mathrm{~s}^{-1}} = 8.16 \\times 10^{17} \\mathrm{~m}^2 \\mathrm{~s}^{-2} \\]\n\nTaking the square root of both sides to find the change in velocity:\n\\[ v = \\sqrt{8.16 \\times 10^{17} \\mathrm{~m}^2 \\mathrm{~s}^{-2}} = 9.01 \\times 10^8 \\mathrm{~m} \\mathrm{~s}^{-1} \\]\n\nFinally, we convert the change in velocity from meters per second to centimeters per second:\n\\[ v_{\\text{cm} \\mathrm{s}^{-1}} = 9.01 \\times 10^8 \\mathrm{~m} \\mathrm{~s}^{-1} \\times \\frac{1000 \\mathrm{~cm}}{1 \\mathrm{~m}} = 9.01 \\times 10^6 \\mathrm{~cm} \\mathrm{s}^{-1} \\]\n\nTherefore, the change in the velocity of the helium atom after the photon absorption is $\\boxed{9.01 \\times 10^6 \\mathrm{~cm} \\mathrm{s}^{-1}}$.",
