            "To solve this problem, we will use the principle of inclusion-exclusion to find the number of people who have at most one symptom.\n\nFirst, let's find the total number of people who have at least one symptom:\n\nTotal people with at least one symptom = Total people with fever + Total people with cough + Total people with breathing problem - Total people with fever and cough - Total people with fever and breathing problem - Total people with cough and breathing problem + Total people with all three symptoms\n\nTotal people with at least one symptom = 900 - 30 = 870\n\nNow, let's find the number of people who have at least two symptoms:\n\nTotal people with at least two symptoms = Total people with fever and cough + Total people with fever and breathing problem + Total people with cough and breathing problem - Total people with fever, cough and breathing problem\n\nTotal people with at least two symptoms = (190 + 220 + 220) - 30 = 870 - 30 = 840\n\nNow, let's find the number of people who have all three symptoms:\n\nTotal people with all three symptoms = Total people with fever, cough and breathing problem\n\nTotal people with all three symptoms = 30\n\nNow, let's find the number of people who have at most one symptom:\n\nTotal people with at most one symptom = Total people with at least one symptom - Total people with at least two symptoms - Total people with all three symptoms\n\nTotal people with at most one symptom = 870 - 840 - 30 = 0\n\nSince the number of people with at most one symptom is 0, it means that all people have at least two symptoms.\n\nTherefore, the probability that a person has at most one symptom is:\n\nProbability = Total people with at least one symptom / Total people\n\nProbability = 870 / 900\n\nProbability = 0.95\n\nSo, the probability that a person has at most one symptom is 0.95.",
