          "question": "Let $\\bar{z}$ denote the complex conjugate of a complex number $z$ and let $i=\\sqrt{-1}$. In the set of complex numbers,what is the number of distinct roots of the equation\n\n\\[\n\\bar{z}-z^{2}=i\\left(\\bar{z}+z^{2}\\right)\n\\]?",
          "model_outputs": [
            "\n1. **Rewrite the Equation:**\n   \n   Given the equation:\n   \\[\n   \\bar{z} - z^2 = i(\\bar{z} + z^2)\n   \\]\n   \n   We can rearrange it to:\n   \\[\n   \\bar{z} - z^2 - i\\bar{z} - i z^2 = 0\n   \\]\n   \n   Combine like terms:\n   \\[\n   \\bar{z} - i\\bar{z} - z^2 - i z^2 = 0\n   \\]\n   \n   Factor out the common terms:\n   \\[\n   \\bar{z}(1 - i) - z^2(1 + i) = 0\n   \\]\n\n2. **Express $\\bar{z}$ in Terms of $z$:**\n   \n   Since $\\bar{z} = \\overline{z}$, we can write:\n   \\[\n   \\overline{z}(1 - i) = z^2(1 + i)\n   \\]\n   \n   Solve for $\\overline{z}$:\n   \\[\n   \\overline{z} = \\frac{z^2(1 + i)}{1 - i}\n   \\]\n   \n   To simplify, multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n   \\[\n   \\overline{z} = \\frac{z^2(1 + i)(1 + i)}{(1 - i)(1 + i)}\n   \\]\n   \n   Simplify the denominator:\n   \\[\n   (1 - i)(1 + i) = 1^2 - i^2 = 1 - (-1) = 2\n   \\]\n   \n   So,\n   \\[\n   \\overline{z} = \\frac{z^2(1 + i)^2}{2}\n   \\]\n   \n   Expand $(1 + i)^2$:\n   \\[\n   (1 + i)^2 = 1^2 + 2 \\cdot 1 \\cdot i + i^2 = 1 + 2i - 1 = 2i\n   \\]\n   \n   Therefore,\n   \\[\n   \\overline{z} = \\frac{z^2 \\cdot 2i}{2} = i z^2\n   \\]\n   \n   Thus,\n   \\[\n   \\overline{z} = i z^2\n   \\]\n\n3. **Substitute Back into the Original Equation:**\n   \n   Substitute $\\overline{z} = i z^2$ into the original equation:\n   \\[\n   i z^2 - z^2 = i(i z^2 + z^2)\n   \\]\n   \n   Simplify both sides:\n   \\[\n   i z^2 - z^2 = i^2 z^2 + i z^2\n   \\]\n   \n   Since $i^2 = -1$, we have:\n   \\[\n   i z^2 - z^2 = -z^2 + i z^2\n   \\]\n   \n   Combine like terms:\n   \\[\n   i z^2 - z^2 = -z^2 + i z^2\n   \\]\n   \n   Factor out $z^2$:\n   \\[\n   z^2(i - 1) = -z^2 + i z^2\n   \\]\n   \n   Rearrange the equation:\n   \\[\n   z^2(i - 1) + z^2 = i z^2\n   \\]\n   \n   Factor out $z^2$:\n   \\[\n   z^2(i - 1 + 1) = i z^2\n   \\]\n   \n   Simplify the equation:\n   \\[\n   z^2 = i z^2\n   \\]\n   \n   Subtract $i z^2$ from both sides:\n   \\[\n   z^2 - i z^2 = 0\n   \\]\n   \n   Factor out $z^2$:\n   \\[\n   z^2(1 - i) = 0\n   \\]\n   \n   This equation is satisfied if either $z^2 = 0$ or $1 - i = 0$.\n\n4. **Solve for $z$:**\n   \n   From $z^2 = 0$, we get:\n   \\[\n   z = 0\n   \\]\n   \n   From $1 - i = 0$, we get:\n   \\[\n   1 = i\n   \\]\n   \n   Since $1 = i$ is not true, the only solution is $z = 0$.\n\n5. **Conclusion:**\n   \n   The only solution to the equation is $z = 0$. Therefore, there is only one distinct root.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]",
