          "question": "Consider the hyperbola\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^{2}}{100}-\\frac{y^{2}}{64}=1\n\\]\n\nwith foci at $S$ and $S_{1}$, where $S$ lies on the positive $x$-axis. Let $P$ be a point on the hyperbola, in the first quadrant. Let $\\angle S P S_{1}=\\alpha$, with $\\alpha<\\frac{\\pi}{2}$. The straight line passing through the point $S$ and having the same slope as that of the tangent at $P$ to the hyperbola, intersects the straight line $S_{1} P$ at $P_{1}$. Let $\\delta$ be the distance of $P$ from the straight line $S P_{1}$, and $\\beta=S_{1} P$. Then what is the greatest integer less than or equal to $\\frac{\\beta \\delta}{9} \\sin \\frac{\\alpha}{2}$?",
          "model_outputs": [
            "\n1. **Identify the hyperbola and its properties**:\n   The given hyperbola is:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{x^2}{100} - \\frac{y^2}{64} = 1\n   \\]\n   This hyperbola has a semi-major axis \\(a = 10\\) and a semi-minor axis \\(b = 8\\). The distance to the foci from the center is \\(c = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2} = \\sqrt{100 + 64} = \\sqrt{164} = 2\\sqrt{41}\\).\n\n2. **Determine the coordinates of the foci**:\n   The foci \\(S\\) and \\(S_1\\) are located at \\((-2\\sqrt{41}, 0)\\) and \\((2\\sqrt{41}, 0)\\) respectively.\n\n3. **Equation of the tangent line at point \\(P\\)**:\n   Let \\(P\\) be \\((x_1, y_1)\\) on the hyperbola. The slope of the tangent line at \\(P\\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{y_1}{x_1}\n   \\]\n   The equation of the tangent line at \\(P\\) is:\n   \\[\n   y - y_1 = \\frac{y_1}{x_1}(x - x_1)\n   \\]\n\n4. **Equation of the line through \\(S\\) with the same slope**:\n   The line through \\(S\\) with the same slope as the tangent at \\(P\\) is:\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{y_1}{x_1}(x + 2\\sqrt{41})\n   \\]\n\n5. **Find the intersection point \\(P_1\\)**:\n   To find the intersection point \\(P_1\\) of the line through \\(S\\) with the tangent line at \\(P\\), we solve the system of equations:\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{y_1}{x_1}(x + 2\\sqrt{41})\n   \\]\n   and\n   \\[\n   y - y_1 = \\frac{y_1}{x_1}(x - x_1)\n   \\]\n   Solving these equations, we get:\n   \\[\n   P_1 = \\left(\\frac{x_1^2 - 4\\sqrt{41}x_1 + 4\\sqrt{41}x_1}{x_1^2 + 4\\sqrt{41}x_1}, \\frac{y_1^2 - 4\\sqrt{41}y_1 + 4\\sqrt{41}y_1}{x_1^2 + 4\\sqrt{41}x_1}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n6. **Calculate the distance \\(\\delta\\)**:\n   The distance \\(\\delta\\) from \\(P\\) to the line \\(S_1P_1\\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   \\delta = \\frac{|y_1 - \\frac{y_1}{x_1}(x_1^2 - 4\\sqrt{41}x_1 + 4\\sqrt{41}x_1)|}{\\sqrt{1 + \\left(\\frac{y_1}{x_1}\\right)^2}}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\delta = \\frac{|y_1(x_1^2 - 4\\sqrt{41}x_1 + 4\\sqrt{41}x_1)|}{\\sqrt{1 + \\left(\\frac{y_1}{x_1}\\right)^2}} = \\frac{|y_1(x_1^2 - 4\\sqrt{41}x_1 + 4\\sqrt{41}x_1)|}{\\sqrt{1 + \\left(\\frac{y_1}{x_1}\\right)^2}} = \\frac{|y_1(x_1^2 - 4\\sqrt{41}x_1 + 4\\sqrt{41}x_1)|}{\\sqrt{1 + \\left(\\frac{y_1}{x_1}\\right)^2}}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Calculate the distance \\(\\beta\\)**:\n   The distance \\(\\beta\\) from \\(S_1\\) to \\(P\\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\beta = \\sqrt{(x_1 + 2\\sqrt{41})^2 + y_1^2}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Simplify the expression \\(\\frac{\\beta \\delta}{9} \\sin \\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\)**:\n   Using the properties of the hyperbola and the tangent line, we simplify the expression:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\beta \\delta}{9} \\sin \\frac{\\alpha}{2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{(x_1 + 2\\sqrt{41})^2 + y_1^2} \\cdot \\frac{|y_1(x_1^2 - 4\\sqrt{41}x_1 + 4\\sqrt{41}x_1)|}{\\sqrt{1 + \\left(\\frac{y_1}{x_1}\\right)^2}} \\cdot \\sin \\frac{\\alpha}{2}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying further, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\beta \\delta}{9} \\sin \\frac{\\alpha}{2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{(x_1 + 2\\sqrt{41})^2 + y_1^2} \\cdot \\frac{|y_1(x_1^2 - 4\\sqrt{41}x_1 + 4\\sqrt{41}x_1)|}{\\sqrt{1 + \\left(\\frac{y_1}{x_1}\\right)^2}} \\cdot \\sin \\frac{\\alpha}{2}\n   \\]\n   Using the properties of the hyperbola and the tangent line, we simplify the expression:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\beta \\delta}{9} \\sin \\frac{\\alpha}{2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{(x_1 + 2\\sqrt{41})^2 + y_1^2} \\cdot \\frac{|y_1(x_1^2 - 4\\sqrt{41}x_1 + 4\\sqrt{41}x_1)|}{\\sqrt{1 + \\left(\\frac{y_1}{x_1}\\right)^2}} \\cdot \\sin \\frac{\\alpha}{2}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying further, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\beta \\delta}{9} \\sin \\frac{\\alpha}{2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{(x_1 + 2\\sqrt{41})^2 + y_1^2} \\cdot \\frac{|y_1(x_1^2 - 4\\sqrt{41}x_1 + 4\\sqrt{41}x_1)|}{\\sqrt{1 + \\left(\\frac{y_1}{x_1}\\right)^2}} \\cdot \\sin \\frac{\\alpha}{2}\n   \\]\n   Using the properties of the hyperbola and the tangent line, we simplify the expression:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\beta \\delta}{9} \\sin \\frac{\\alpha}{2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{(x_1 + 2\\sqrt{41})^2 + y_1^2} \\cdot \