          "question": "A closed container contains a homogeneous mixture of two moles of an ideal monatomic gas $(\\gamma=5 / 3)$ and one mole of an ideal diatomic gas $(\\gamma=7 / 5)$. Here, $\\gamma$ is the ratio of the specific heats at constant pressure and constant volume of an ideal gas. The gas mixture does a work of 66 Joule when heated at constant pressure. What is the change in its internal energy in Joule.",
          "model_outputs": [
            "\n1. **Define the variables and given information:**\n   - Let \\( n_1 \\) be the number of moles of the monatomic gas.\n   - Let \\( n_2 \\) be the number of moles of the diatomic gas.\n   - The specific heat capacities at constant pressure (\\( C_p \\)) for the monatomic gas and the diatomic gas are \\( C_p_1 = \\frac{5}{3} \\) J/mol·K and \\( C_p_2 = \\frac{7}{5} \\) J/mol·K, respectively.\n   - The total number of moles of the gas mixture is \\( n = n_1 + n_2 \\).\n   - The work done by the gas mixture when heated at constant pressure is \\( W = 66 \\) J.\n\n2. **Calculate the heat transferred to the monatomic gas:**\n   - The heat transferred to the monatomic gas is given by \\( Q_1 = n_1 C_p_1 T \\), where \\( T \\) is the temperature in Kelvin.\n   - The heat transferred to the diatomic gas is given by \\( Q_2 = n_2 C_p_2 T \\).\n\n3. **Set up the equation for the total work done:**\n   - The total work done by the gas mixture is the sum of the work done on the monatomic gas and the work done on the diatomic gas.\n   - Therefore, \\( W = Q_1 + Q_2 \\).\n\n4. **Substitute the given values into the equation:**\n   - \\( 66 = n_1 \\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right) T + n_2 \\left(\\frac{7}{5}\\right) T \\).\n\n5. **Solve for the temperature \\( T \\):**\n   - \\( 66 = \\frac{5}{3} n_1 T + \\frac{7}{5} n_2 T \\).\n   - \\( 66 = T \\left(\\frac{5}{3} n_1 + \\frac{7}{5} n_2\\right) \\).\n   - \\( T = \\frac{66}{\\frac{5}{3} n_1 + \\frac{7}{5} n_2} \\).\n\n6. **Calculate the change in internal energy:**\n   - The change in internal energy \\( \\Delta U \\) is given by \\( \\Delta U = n_1 C_v T + n_2 C_v T \\), where \\( C_v \\) is the molar specific heat at constant volume.\n   - Since the specific heat capacities are given at constant pressure, we use \\( C_p \\) for the monatomic gas and \\( C_v \\) for the diatomic gas.\n   - \\( C_v = C_p + C_v \\), where \\( C_v \\) is the molar specific heat at constant volume.\n   - \\( C_v = \\frac{5}{3} + \\frac{7}{5} 