ing further, we get:\n     \\[\n     f'(x) = 4 \\left(x-\\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\left(x-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\left(x-\\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\cdot 2x\n     \\]\n   - Therefore, the derivative is:\n     \\[\n     f'(x) = 8x \\left(x-\\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\left(x-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\left(x-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\n     \\]\n   - At $x=0$, the derivative is:\n     \\[\n     f'(0) = 8 \\cdot 0 \\left(0-\\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\left(0-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\left(0-\\frac{1}{4}\\right) = 0\n     \\]\n   - Since the derivative is $0$ at $x=0$, the function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x=0$.\n\n3. **Conclusion**:\n   - The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$ and differentiable at $x=0$.\n   - Therefore, the correct answer is:\n     \\[\n     \\boxed{\\text{B}}\n     \\]"
