            "\n1. **Identify the Differential Equation**:\n   The given differential equation is:\n   \\[\n   \\left(x^{2}-5\\right) \\frac{d y}{d x}-2 x y=-2 x\\left(x^{2}-5\\right)^{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Rewrite the Equation**:\n   Rearrange the equation to isolate $\\frac{d y}{d x}$:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{d y}{d x} = \\frac{2 x y + 2 x\\left(x^{2}-5\\right)^{2}}{x^{2}-5}\n   \\]\n   Simplify the right-hand side:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{d y}{d x} = \\frac{2 x y + 2 x\\left(x^{2}-5\\right)^{2}}{x^{2}-5} = 2 y + 2 x\\left(x^{2}-5\\right)\n   \\]\n\n3. **Integrate to Find $y(x)$**:\n   Integrate both sides with respect to $x$:\n   \\[\n   \\int \\frac{d y}{d x} dx = \\int \\left(2 y + 2 x\\left(x^{2}-5\\right)\\right) dx\n   \\]\n   This gives:\n   \\[\n   y(x) = \\frac{1}{2} y(x) + x\\left(x^{2}-5\\right) + C\n   \\]\n   where $C$ is the constant of integration.\n\n4. **Apply the Initial Condition**:\n   Given $y(2) = 7$, substitute $x = 2$ and $y(2) = 7$ into the equation:\n   \\[\n   7 = \\frac{1}{2} y(2) + 2\\left(2^{2}-5\\right) + C\n   \\]\n   Simplify the equation:\n   \\[\n   7 = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 7 + 2\\left(4-5\\right) + C\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   7 = 3.5 - 2 + C\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   C = 7 - 3.5 + 2 = 5.5\n   \\]\n\n5. **Final Solution for $y(x)$**:\n   Substitute $C$ back into the equation:\n   \\[\n   y(x) = \\frac{1}{2} y(x) + x\\left(x^{2}-5\\right) + 5.5\n   \\]\n   Rearrange to solve for $y(x)$:\n   \\[\n   y(x) - \\frac{1}{2} y(x) = x\\left(x^{2}-5\\right) + 5.5\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{2} y(x) = x\\left(x^{2}-5\\right) + 5.5\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y(x) = 2x\\left(x^{2}-5\\right) + 11\n   \\]\n\n6. **Find the Maximum Value**:\n   To find the maximum value of $y(x)$, we need to find the critical points of $y(x)$. Set the derivative of $y(x)$ to zero:\n   \\[\n   y'(x) = 2\\left(x^{2}-5\\right) + 2x\\left(2x\\right) = 2x\\left(x^{2}-5\\right) + 4x^{2}\n   \\]\n   Set $y'(x) = 0$:\n   \\[\n   2x\\left(x^{2}-5\\right) + 4x^{2} = 0\n   \\]\n   Factor out $2x$:\n   \\[