            "To solve this problem, we need to determine the total number of carbon atoms and heteroatoms present in one molecule of compound $\\mathbf{S}$.\n\n1. **Calculate the molar mass of compound $\\mathbf{S}$:**\n   - Carbon (C): $12 \\times 1 = 12$ g/mol\n   - Nitrogen (N): $14 \\times 1 = 14$ g/mol\n   - Oxygen (O): $16 \\times 1 = 16$ g/mol\n   - Bromine (Br): $80 \\times 1 = 80$ g/mol\n   - Chlorine (Cl): $35.5 \\times 1 = 35.5$ g/mol\n\n   The total molar mass of compound $\\mathbf{S}$ is:\n   \\[\n   12 + 14 + 16 + 80 + 35.5 = 157.5 \\text{ g/mol}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the number of carbon atoms in one molecule of $\\mathbf{S}$:**\n   - Since the molar mass of $\\mathbf{S}$ is $157.5$ g/mol, and we are considering one molecule, the number of carbon atoms is:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{157.5}{12} = 13.125\n   \\]\n   Since the number of atoms must be an integer, we round down to the nearest whole number, which is $13$ carbon atoms.\n\n3. **Determine the number of heteroatoms in one molecule of $\\mathbf{S}$:**\n   - The heteroatoms present in $\\mathbf{S}$ are nitrogen (N), oxygen (O), bromine (Br), and chlorine (Cl).\n   - The total number of heteroatoms is:\n   \\[\n   1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 4\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the total number of carbon atoms and heteroatoms in one molecule of $\\mathbf{S}$:**\n   - The total number of carbon atoms and heteroatoms is:\n   \\[\n   13 + 4 = 17\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the total number of carbon atoms and heteroatoms present in one molecule of compound $\\mathbf{S}$ is $\\boxed{17}$.",
